    "updated_at": "2026-08-06T18:09:22Z",
    "body": "> I think regardless of whether `javascript:` actually navigates for these elements, that it would be good to make the the sanitizer strip the value, hence why I've added it to the list you mention.\r\n\r\nThanks, I had indeed overlooked that.\r\n\r\n> I think regardless of whether javascript: actually navigates for these elements, that it would be good to make the the sanitizer strip the value,\r\n\r\nI disagree with that, specifically the \"regardless\" part: For any other \"baseline\" decision for sanitizer we were quite keen to limit them to actual script execution. I don't think we did _just in case_ removals in baseline. So we should figure out if MathML's `<a>` navigates to javascript:-URLs and if so we should baseline-sanitize them; if not, then not.\r\n\r\nI'm having a hard time following the spec here... The way it's currently written, would MathML's `<math><mtext><a href=\"javascript:alert(0)\">xxx</a></mtext></math>` be expected to pop an alert when activated or not? If so, then I think we're on the same page with respect to Sanitizer. Although, as expressed [here](https://github.com/w3c/mathml-core/issues/333) and [here](https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/blink-dev/c/kmtYoVZjtZE/m/Fx-bMNyREAAJ) that's not the behaviour I was hoping for.\r\n",
